                    <h1 class="content-title">Issues, Remediation & <span class="gradient-text">Exceptions</span></h1>
                    <p class="content-subtitle">
                        When controls fail, audits reveal gaps, or policies can't be met — IRM's issue management 
                        framework tracks every finding from detection through remediation or exception. Understand 
                        the full lifecycle, risk treatment strategies, and GRC workspaces that tie it all together.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <!-- Issue Overview -->
                <section class="content-section" id="issue-overview">
                    <h2>Issues in IRM</h2>
                    <p>
                        An <strong>Issue</strong> in ServiceNow IRM represents a gap, finding, or deficiency 
                        that needs to be addressed. Issues are the reactive side of risk management — they are 
                        created when something goes wrong (a control fails, an audit identifies a weakness, or 
                        a compliance requirement is not met).
                    </p>

                    <h3>How Issues Get Created</h3>
                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Source</th>
                                    <th>Trigger</th>
                                    <th>Auto-Created?</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Control Indicator Failure</strong></td>
                                    <td>An automated or scripted indicator returns a "Fail" result</td>
                                    <td>Yes — if "auto-create issue" is enabled on the indicator</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Failed Attestation</strong></td>
                                    <td>A control owner attests that a control is ineffective</td>
                                    <td>Yes — system auto-generates an issue linked to the control</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Audit Finding</strong></td>
                                    <td>An auditor documents a finding during an audit engagement</td>
                                    <td>Manual — auditor creates the issue from the audit workbench</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Risk Event</strong></td>
                                    <td>A risk materializes (a security incident, compliance breach, etc.)</td>
                                    <td>Can be automated via Flow Designer or Business Rule</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Manual Reporting</strong></td>
                                    <td>A stakeholder identifies and reports a gap or concern</td>
                                    <td>Manual — user creates the issue directly</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Vendor Assessment</strong></td>
                                    <td>A TPRM assessment reveals vendor non-compliance</td>
                                    <td>Semi-automated — triggered from assessment review</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--important">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Issue Linkage</div>
                        <p>
                            Issues in IRM are always linked to their source context — a specific control, risk, 
                            entity, or audit engagement. This linkage is critical because it enables roll-up 
                            reporting (e.g., "How many open issues does Entity X have?") and ensures that 
                            resolving an issue updates the compliance status of the parent control or risk.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Issue Lifecycle -->
                <section class="content-section" id="issue-lifecycle">
                    <h2>Issue Lifecycle</h2>
                    <p>
                        GRC Issues follow a structured state flow from creation to closure. Each state transition 
                        represents a distinct phase of the investigation and remediation process.
                    </p>

                    <div class="diagram-block">
                        <div class="diagram-title">GRC Issue State Flow</div>
                        <div class="mermaid">
                        stateDiagram-v2
                            [*] --> New
                            New --> Analyze : Assigned for investigation
                            Analyze --> Respond : Root cause identified
                            Respond --> Review : Remediation complete
                            Review --> Closed : Verified effective
                            Review --> Respond : Rework needed
                            Analyze --> Accepted : Risk accepted
                            Accepted --> Closed : Acceptance approved
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h3>State Descriptions</h3>
                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>State</th>
                                    <th>Description</th>
                                    <th>Owner</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>New</strong></td>
                                    <td>Issue is created and awaiting triage. Assignment group and priority are set.</td>
                                    <td>Issue Creator / System</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Analyze</strong></td>
                                    <td>Issue is being investigated. Root cause analysis, impact assessment, and stakeholder communication occur here.</td>
                                    <td>Issue Assignee</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Respond</strong></td>
                                    <td>Active remediation. Remediation tasks are created and tracked. This is where the actual fix happens.</td>
                                    <td>Remediation Owner(s)</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Review</strong></td>
                                    <td>Remediation is complete and under review. A manager or auditor validates that the fix is effective.</td>
                                    <td>Issue Manager / Auditor</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Accepted</strong></td>
                                    <td>The risk is accepted rather than remediated (requires formal approval with justification and expiration date).</td>
                                    <td>Risk Owner / Senior Leadership</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Closed</strong></td>
                                    <td>Issue is resolved (remediated or formally accepted). The parent control/risk status is updated.</td>
                                    <td>System / Issue Manager</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Remediation Tasks -->
                <section class="content-section" id="remediation-tasks">
                    <h2>Remediation Tasks</h2>
                    <p>
                        Remediation tasks are the <strong>actionable work items</strong> created to resolve 
                        a GRC issue. A single issue can have multiple remediation tasks assigned to different 
                        teams or individuals, allowing parallel workstreams for complex remediation efforts.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Remediation Task Properties</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Parent Issue</strong> — Every remediation task is linked to exactly one GRC Issue.</li>
                        <li><strong>Assignment</strong> — Can be assigned to a specific user or assignment group.</li>
                        <li><strong>Due Date</strong> — Calculated based on issue severity and organizational SLAs.</li>
                        <li><strong>Evidence</strong> — Remediation owners can attach evidence (screenshots, config exports, logs) to demonstrate that the fix was implemented.</li>
                        <li><strong>State Flow</strong> — Open → In Progress → Complete → Verified.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <div class="diagram-block">
                        <div class="diagram-title">Issue to Remediation Task Breakdown</div>
                        <div class="mermaid">
                        flowchart TB
                            ISSUE["GRC Issue<br/>Firewall Rules Non-Compliant"]
                            RT1["Remediation Task 1<br/>Update firewall rules<br/>(Network Team)"]
                            RT2["Remediation Task 2<br/>Update documentation<br/>(Security Team)"]
                            RT3["Remediation Task 3<br/>Re-run compliance scan<br/>(GRC Team)"]

                            ISSUE --> RT1
                            ISSUE --> RT2
                            ISSUE --> RT3

                            style ISSUE fill:transparent,stroke:#ef4444,color:#d1d5db
                            style RT1 fill:transparent,stroke:#3b82f6,color:#d1d5db
                            style RT2 fill:transparent,stroke:#f59e0b,color:#d1d5db
                            style RT3 fill:transparent,stroke:#10b981,color:#d1d5db
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Issue Closure Gate</div>
                        <p>
                            An issue cannot move to the <strong>Closed</strong> state until all of its 
                            remediation tasks are marked as <strong>Complete</strong> or <strong>Cancelled</strong>. 
                            This gate ensures that no issue is prematurely closed while work remains outstanding. 
                            This is enforced by an OOTB Business Rule on the <code>sn_grc_issue</code> table.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Exception Management -->
                <section class="content-section" id="exception-management">
                    <h2>Exception Management</h2>
                    <p>
                        Sometimes a policy or control requirement <strong>cannot be met</strong> — perhaps 
                        due to technical limitations, cost constraints, or business priorities. Rather than 
                        ignoring the non-compliance, organizations use <strong>exceptions</strong> to formally 
                        document, approve, and track these deviations.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Exception Lifecycle</h3>
                    <div class="diagram-block">
                        <div class="diagram-title">Exception Request Flow</div>
                        <div class="mermaid">
                        flowchart LR
                            A["Request<br/>Exception"] --> B["Justify &<br/>Document"]
                            B --> C["Risk Assessment<br/>(Impact Analysis)"]
                            C --> D["Manager<br/>Review"]
                            D -->|"Approved"| E["Active Exception<br/>(Time-Bound)"]
                            D -->|"Denied"| F["Must Remediate"]
                            E --> G["Expiration<br/>Review"]
                            G -->|"Renew"| D
                            G -->|"Resolve"| H["Exception<br/>Closed"]

                            style A fill:transparent,stroke:#3b82f6,color:#d1d5db
                            style B fill:transparent,stroke:#8b5cf6,color:#d1d5db
                            style C fill:transparent,stroke:#f59e0b,color:#d1d5db
                            style D fill:transparent,stroke:#6b7280,color:#d1d5db
                            style E fill:transparent,stroke:#10b981,color:#d1d5db
                            style F fill:transparent,stroke:#ef4444,color:#d1d5db
                            style G fill:transparent,stroke:#f59e0b,color:#d1d5db
                            style H fill:transparent,stroke:#6b7280,color:#d1d5db
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Exception Requirements</h3>
                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Requirement</th>
                                    <th>Description</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Business Justification</strong></td>
                                    <td>Clear explanation of why the requirement cannot be met and the business rationale.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Risk Assessment</strong></td>
                                    <td>Quantified assessment of the risk introduced by the exception (impact, likelihood, residual risk).</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Compensating Controls</strong></td>
                                    <td>Alternative measures implemented to mitigate the risk (e.g., additional monitoring, manual checks).</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Expiration Date</strong></td>
                                    <td>Exceptions must be time-bound (typically 6-12 months). Permanent exceptions are strongly discouraged.</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Approval Chain</strong></td>
                                    <td>Minimum two levels of approval — the control owner's manager and the risk committee or CISO.</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Risk Treatment Plans -->
                <section class="content-section" id="risk-treatment">
                    <h2>Risk Treatment Plans</h2>
                    <p>
                        While issues and remediation are <strong>reactive</strong> (fixing something that's 
                        already broken), <strong>risk treatment plans</strong> are <strong>proactive</strong> — 
                        strategic responses to identified risks before they materialize into incidents.
                    </p>

                    <h3>The Four Treatment Strategies</h3>
                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Strategy</th>
                                    <th>Description</th>
                                    <th>When to Use</th>
                                    <th>Example</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><span style="color:#10b981;font-weight:600;">Mitigate</span></td>
                                    <td>Implement controls to reduce the likelihood or impact of the risk</td>
                                    <td>Risk is within appetite after mitigation; cost of controls is justified</td>
                                    <td>Deploy MFA to reduce unauthorized access risk</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><span style="color:#3b82f6;font-weight:600;">Transfer</span></td>
                                    <td>Shift the financial impact to a third party (insurance, outsourcing)</td>
                                    <td>Risk impact is primarily financial; transfer is cost-effective</td>
                                    <td>Purchase cyber insurance for data breach liability</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><span style="color:#ef4444;font-weight:600;">Avoid</span></td>
                                    <td>Eliminate the risk entirely by discontinuing the activity that creates it</td>
                                    <td>Risk exceeds appetite and cannot be adequately mitigated</td>
                                    <td>Stop collecting unnecessary PII to avoid privacy risk</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><span style="color:#f59e0b;font-weight:600;">Accept</span></td>
                                    <td>Acknowledge the risk and take no additional action (requires formal approval)</td>
                                    <td>Risk is within appetite; cost of mitigation exceeds potential impact</td>
                                    <td>Accept minor availability risk for non-critical internal tool</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <p>
                        In ServiceNow, risk treatment is managed through <strong>Risk Response Tasks</strong> 
                        linked to specific risk records. These tasks track the implementation of the chosen 
                        treatment strategy and provide audit evidence of risk management decisions.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <!-- GRC Workspace & Dashboards -->
                <section class="content-section" id="grc-workspace">
                    <h2>GRC Workspace & Dashboards</h2>
                    <p>
                        ServiceNow provides specialized <strong>workspaces</strong> that give risk and compliance 
                        teams a unified, single-pane-of-glass view for managing their daily work.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Key Workspaces</h3>
                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Workspace</th>
                                    <th>Primary Users</th>
                                    <th>Key Features</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Risk Workspace</strong></td>
                                    <td>Risk Managers, Risk Analysts</td>
                                    <td>Risk register, risk assessments, treatment plans, risk heat maps, entity risk rollup</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Issue Workspace</strong></td>
                                    <td>Issue Owners, Remediation Teams</td>
                                    <td>Open issues, remediation task tracking, SLA compliance, issue aging reports</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Compliance Workspace</strong></td>
                                    <td>Compliance Officers, Control Owners</td>
                                    <td>Control status overview, attestation management, policy exceptions, authority document tracking</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Audit Workspace</strong></td>
                                    <td>Internal Auditors</td>
                                    <td>Audit planning, engagement tracking, evidence management, finding documentation</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Dashboard Visualizations</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Risk Heat Maps</strong> — 2D matrix plotting risks by impact (Y-axis) and likelihood (X-axis). Color-coded by risk severity.</li>
                        <li><strong>Control Effectiveness Donut Charts</strong> — Percentage breakdown of compliant, non-compliant, and untested controls.</li>
                        <li><strong>Issue Aging Bar Charts</strong> — Time-based view showing how long issues have been open, grouped by severity.</li>
                        <li><strong>Trend Lines</strong> — Performance Analytics trend charts showing risk posture changes over time.</li>
                        <li><strong>Entity Risk Rollup</strong> — Aggregated risk scores per entity (business unit, application, vendor) with drill-down capability.</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <!-- Developer Patterns -->
                <section class="content-section" id="developer-issues">
                    <h2>Developer Patterns</h2>
                    <p>
                        Developers working with IRM issues, remediation, and exceptions commonly encounter 
                        these implementation and customization scenarios.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Common Development Tasks</h3>
                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Task</th>
                                    <th>Approach</th>
                                    <th>Key Tables</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Auto-create issues from external findings (pen test reports, etc.)</td>
                                    <td>Scripted REST API that creates <code>sn_grc_issue</code> records with proper linkage to controls/entities</td>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_issue</code>, <code>sn_compliance_control</code></td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Custom remediation SLA calculation</td>
                                    <td>Business Rule on <code>sn_grc_issue</code> that calculates due date based on severity, entity criticality, and regulatory requirements</td>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_issue</code>, <code>sn_grc_remediation_task</code></td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Exception expiration notifications</td>
                                    <td>Flow Designer with scheduled trigger that queries active exceptions nearing expiration and sends notifications</td>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_exception</code>, Flow Designer</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Custom risk heat map widget</td>
                                    <td>Service Portal widget using PA (Performance Analytics) API to render dynamic heat map data</td>
                                    <td><code>sn_risk_risk</code>, PA API</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>Integrate issues with ITSM incidents</td>
                                    <td>Flow Designer that creates a linked Incident when a critical GRC issue is created, enabling ITSM teams to track the technical fix</td>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_issue</code>, <code>incident</code></td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Code Pattern: Auto-Create Issue from External Finding</h3>
                    <div class="code-block">
                        <div class="code-header">
                            <span class="code-lang">JavaScript — Scripted REST API for Issue Ingestion</span>
                            <button class="code-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy code to clipboard">Copy</button>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-body">
                            <pre><code class="language-javascript">// Scripted REST API: POST /api/x_custom/grc_issues
(function process(request, response) {
    var body = request.body.data;
    
    // Validate required fields
    if (!body.finding_title || !body.control_sys_id) {
        response.setStatus(400);
        response.setBody({
            error: 'Missing required fields: finding_title, control_sys_id'
        });
        return;
    }
    
    var issueGR = new GlideRecord('sn_grc_issue');
    issueGR.initialize();
    issueGR.setValue('short_description', body.finding_title);
    issueGR.setValue('description', body.finding_details || '');
    issueGR.setValue('priority', body.severity || '3');
    issueGR.setValue('state', 'new');
    
    // Link to the control
    issueGR.setValue('control', body.control_sys_id);
    
    // Get the entity from the control
    var controlGR = new GlideRecord('sn_compliance_control');
    if (controlGR.get(body.control_sys_id)) {
        issueGR.setValue('profile', controlGR.getValue('profile'));
    }
    
    // Set source for traceability
    issueGR.setValue('source', 'External Pen Test');
    
    var sysId = issueGR.insert();
    
    response.setStatus(201);
    response.setBody({
        result: {
            sys_id: sysId,
            number: issueGR.getValue('number'),
            message: 'GRC Issue created successfully'
        }
    });
})(request, response);</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--warning">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Avoid Using Table API for GRC Records</div>
                        <p>
                            Do <strong>not</strong> use the standard Table API (<code>/api/now/table/sn_grc_issue</code>) 
                            to create GRC records from external systems. The Table API bypasses critical UI policies, 
                            state flow validation, and business rules that ensure data integrity. Always use 
                            <strong>Scripted REST APIs</strong> or <strong>IntegrationHub</strong> to enforce 
                            business logic and maintain proper audit trails.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

